<%# locals: (minor:, guardian: nil) %>
<%#
  Fuime Guardian Agreement, version 2026-09-11-v4.

  Why a v4 the same day as v3: two sessions produced a v3 in parallel. The one
  that reached main first (2026-09-11-v3, PR #99) was deployed, so it may have
  been signed and is immutable. It still says "software platform, not a bank"
  (L5 prescribes the standing disclosure), and its §2 and §4 say consent gates
  whether the minor can "create or run" a business and that revocation means
  they "will no longer be able to operate" — both false under merchant-of-record,
  where the guardian gates payouts. v4 keeps v3's legal framing (Ninth Street
  Labs, LLC doing business as Fuime is the seller of record; the guardian is the
  legal payee and clawback obligor) and corrects the rest.

  Changed from v2 and v3:

  - Section 5 described Fuime as "a software platform, not a bank". L5 (CLAUDE.md,
    LEGAL_RESEARCH.md §7) prescribes the standing disclosure "Fuime is a financial
    technology company, not a bank", and this is the one document a parent signs,
    so it now carries that disclosure verbatim — sentences 1–3 exactly as
    LEGAL_RESEARCH.md §7 gives them. Sentence 4 is adapted, deliberately: §7's
    "Venture accounts are opened and owned by a parent or legal guardian" is
    false under merchant-of-record, where the minor may open the venture and
    sell before any guardian has accepted, and this document is addressed to a
    named guardian rather than to the public. So it reads "owned by you, a
    parent or legal guardian" and drops "opened".

  - Section 5 was written for the Stripe Connect posture: "a venture's payment
    account is opened in your name with Stripe", "paid out to a bank account you
    own", and "does not hold that money at any point". Production runs
    merchant-of-record, where there is no per-venture Stripe account, Fuime is
    the seller of record, and Fuime pays the business what it has earned. Every
    one of those three sentences was false for the guardians signing it.

  - Sections 2 and 4 said consent gates whether the minor can "create or run" a
    business. Under merchant-of-record the guardian gates *payouts*: a minor can
    list and sell before a parent has accepted, and nothing is paid out until one
    has. The text now says what consent and revocation actually control.

  - The guardian gate is not absolute in the code, so the agreement does not
    promise that it is. Event#payout_setup_blockers skips it for a venture inside
    a school programme (Event#institutionally_sponsored?), and User#needs_guardian?
    is false after an admin waiver (User#waive_guardian_requirement!, an audited
    override with no parent signature behind it). Both exceptions are stated once,
    in section 5, and sections 2 and 4 refer to it rather than restating the rule
    without them.

  - The gate runs when the weekly run is generated (Fuime::PayableAssessment), not
    when it is approved or marked paid (Fuime::PayoutBatchService). A revocation
    after Wednesday's generation does not pull that line from Friday's payment, so
    section 4 says a payout already scheduled or released may still be sent,
    instead of "takes effect immediately: no money can be paid out".

  This partial deliberately does NOT branch on Fuime::Features.merchant_of_record?
  or any other runtime flag. The text a guardian signed must be reproducible
  from the stored version alone, years later, on a codebase whose flags have
  moved. So every sentence below is written to be true under merchant-of-record
  AND under guardian-owned Connect accounts, and the mechanics that differ
  between the two are left to the Terms of Service, which are referenced.

  This file IS the agreement text for that version. It is rendered both when a
  guardian signs and, afterwards, on the permanent record page — so a guardian
  can always re-read exactly what they agreed to.

  Do not edit this file to change the terms. Add a NEW versioned partial and
  bump Guardianship::CURRENT_AGREEMENT_VERSION instead: guardianships store the
  version they were signed under, and editing this text in place would silently
  rewrite what past guardians are recorded as having accepted.

  The version below is written as a literal, not as
  Guardianship::CURRENT_AGREEMENT_VERSION — see v2's header for why.
%>

<div class="guardian-agreement">
  <p>
    This agreement is between
    <strong><%= Rails.configuration.constants.legal_entity_name %></strong>,
    doing business as Fuime (&ldquo;Fuime&rdquo;, &ldquo;we&rdquo;), and you,
    the parent or legal guardian of
    <strong><%= minor.name.presence || minor.email %></strong> (&ldquo;the minor&rdquo;).
    By signing, you agree to the following.
  </p>

  <h4>1. You are the responsible adult</h4>
  <p>
    You confirm that you are at least 18 years old and that you are the parent
    or legal guardian of the minor. You accept legal responsibility for the
    minor's use of Fuime, including any business they operate through it.
  </p>
  <p>
    You are the person bound by this agreement. The minor is not, and cannot be
    — a contract signed by a minor is generally voidable, which is why the adult
    signs. Where Fuime's <%= link_to "Terms of Service", terms_path %> place an
    obligation on the account, that obligation is yours.
  </p>

  <h4>2. You consent to the minor's account</h4>
  <p>
    You give permission for the minor to hold a Fuime account and to operate a
    business through it. The minor runs the business day to day; you are the
    adult responsible for it and the owner of record of its account on Fuime.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your consent is what allows money to be paid out of that business. Without
    a parent or legal guardian on the account, no money is paid out of the
    business except as section 5 describes, and Fuime may limit what the minor
    can do on Fuime until a guardian has accepted.
  </p>

  <h4>3. You can see everything</h4>
  <p>
    You will have visibility into the minor's business activity on Fuime,
    including every sale, what the business has earned and been paid, and the
    people on their team. This is not optional and cannot be turned off by the
    minor.
  </p>

  <h4>4. You can withdraw consent at any time</h4>
  <p>
    You may revoke this guardianship whenever you choose, from your account or
    by contacting Fuime support. Revoking takes effect as soon as you do it. From
    then on, Fuime will not schedule any new payout of the minor's business until
    a parent or legal guardian is on the account again, except as section 5
    describes, and Fuime may limit or suspend what the minor can do on Fuime. A
    payout that was already scheduled or released when you revoked may still be
    sent. Money the business has already earned is not forfeited by revoking; it
    remains subject to the terms below.
  </p>

  <h4>5. Money and taxes</h4>
  <p>
    Fuime is a financial technology company, not a bank. Fuime does not hold
    deposits and does not offer FDIC-insured products. Payments are processed by
    Stripe. The minor's venture account is owned by you, a parent or legal
    guardian; the minor operates it with your oversight.
  </p>
  <p>
    Money the business earns is paid out only to a payout destination that you
    set up. Only a parent or legal guardian on the account (or Fuime, at a
    guardian's request) can add or change that destination. No money is paid out
    of the business without a parent or legal guardian on the account, with two
    exceptions. Where the business runs inside a school programme Fuime has
    approved, the school is the responsible party for payouts. And Fuime may, by
    a recorded decision of a named Fuime administrator, override the requirement
    for a parent or legal guardian for a particular user; Fuime records who made
    that decision and when, and can restore the requirement. The minor cannot
    move money out of the business on their own. Fuime pays what the business has
    earned to that destination on a regular schedule, and any payout that needs a
    decision is yours to make, not the minor's.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where Fuime is the seller of record for the business's sales, the customer
    buys from <%= Rails.configuration.constants.legal_entity_name %>. Fuime
    collects the customer's payment, keeps its fee, and owes the rest to the
    business as a payable, which it pays to the destination above after any
    fees, refunds and disputes. You are the legal payee and the clawback
    obligor: refunds and disputes on the business's sales may reduce what it is
    paid, and where they exceed it, the shortfall is yours. Fuime's
    <%= link_to "Terms of Service", terms_path %> govern sales made through
    Fuime, including refunds and disputes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Money moving through a business on Fuime may create tax obligations. Fuime,
    or its payment processor, may be required to report payments made to the
    business to tax authorities, and may ask you for taxpayer information before
    money is paid out. Fuime provides records and summaries to help, but Fuime
    does not give tax or legal advice, and you are responsible for the minor's
    tax filings to the extent the law assigns them to you.
  </p>

  <h4>6. What this agreement is not</h4>
  <p>
    Accepting this agreement records your consent. It is not identity
    verification, and Fuime does not currently verify that a person accepting a
    guardian invitation is the minor's actual parent or legal guardian. Do not
    accept this invitation on behalf of someone else.
  </p>

  <p class="muted text-sm">
    Agreement version 2026-09-11-v4, between you and
    <%= Rails.configuration.constants.legal_entity_name %>.
    Questions? Email <%= mail_to "support@fuime.com" %>.
  </p>
</div>
